  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find all values of $k$ such that $y=e^{kx}$ is a solution to the differential equation $y''+5y'=6y$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=1$ or $k=-6$.\n\nSubstituting the derivatives gives $k^2+5k-6=0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*} y'&=ke^{kx}\\\\ y''&=k^2e^{kx}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute into $y''+5y'=6y$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} k^2e^{kx}+5ke^{kx}&=6e^{kx}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $e^{kx}>0$, divide by $e^{kx}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} k^2+5k&=6\\\\ k^2+5k-6&=0\\\\ (k-1)(k+6)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth roots satisfy the resulting equation, so $k=1$ or $k=-6$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
